WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.334 --> 00:00:03.167
Oh boy.

00:00:04.177 --> 00:00:06.404
Back in the dawn of
aviation, in the 1920s,

00:00:06.404 --> 00:00:09.579
wing-walking was an
incredibly dangerous stunt.

00:00:09.579 --> 00:00:11.574
Daredevils would get out
of the seat in their plane

00:00:11.574 --> 00:00:13.570
and just clamber around the wings

00:00:13.570 --> 00:00:16.103
with nothing but their own strength.

00:00:16.103 --> 00:00:17.651
Maybe there'd be a pole at the top

00:00:17.651 --> 00:00:19.257
they could attach to
once they were up there,

00:00:19.257 --> 00:00:22.205
but mostly it was just
them and their strength

00:00:22.205 --> 00:00:24.733
against 100mph wind.

00:00:24.740 --> 00:00:28.460
Now there are still a very small number
of folks in the world

00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:30.880
who do wing-walking that way,

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:33.100
but in the 21st century,

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:35.680
wing-walking is a little bit different.

00:00:40.155 --> 00:00:40.988
Hahaha!

00:00:41.987 --> 00:00:42.820
Oh, yes!

00:00:44.557 --> 00:00:45.390
Oh, wow.

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:52.920
- We go at roughly from 80 to 160mph.
Various, in between that.

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:56.640
And we'll pull up to 4g,
so it's a huge strain on our bodies

00:00:56.644 --> 00:00:58.715
and we're doing manoeuvres,
so we'll lift our legs up,

00:00:58.715 --> 00:01:01.014
we can do handstands and
we'll also move around.

00:01:01.014 --> 00:01:03.319
It's like resistance training,
it's really hard work.

00:01:03.319 --> 00:01:05.943
In the loop, we'll get
to about 4g at the bottom

00:01:05.943 --> 00:01:07.405
and then as soon as we
go over the top

00:01:07.405 --> 00:01:09.300
we start waving, cause that's
the nice weightless bit,

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:12.349
so it's easier to start then, than start
when you're going really quickly.

00:01:12.349 --> 00:01:14.211
You have about a month
of intense training.

00:01:14.211 --> 00:01:15.797
You practise everything
on the ground first

00:01:15.797 --> 00:01:18.630
and then you just go up in the air
and do it repetitively.

00:01:18.630 --> 00:01:21.381
You fly everyday, all day,
so it's absolutely knackering.

00:01:21.381 --> 00:01:23.611
- At Eastbourne Airshow, we take off

00:01:23.611 --> 00:01:26.064
from an airfield near Brighton
and we fly along the coast

00:01:26.064 --> 00:01:28.360
for 20 minutes and then they climb up onto the wing,

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:29.843
so they climb up in flight.

00:01:29.843 --> 00:01:32.720
On the transit flight, the procedure for them to climb onto the wing

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:36.015
is for them to unstrap their
main seat harness, but at all times

00:01:36.020 --> 00:01:39.760
they have a carabiner which
slides up and down the wire.

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:43.100
Their cable is about 60cm long,

00:01:43.104 --> 00:01:47.047
so they can never be more than 60cm away
from the centre line of the aircraft.

00:01:47.047 --> 00:01:48.501
Once they get up onto the wing,

00:01:48.501 --> 00:01:51.309
the carabiner is then behind them,
still attached,

00:01:51.309 --> 00:01:53.529
and then they strap themselves
into the five point harness,

00:01:53.529 --> 00:01:55.903
which is on the swivel rig on the wing

00:01:55.903 --> 00:01:59.073
and at that point, of course, then they have
two methods of attachment.

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:01.740
That's totally different to the 1920s!

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:05.380
 

00:02:05.380 --> 00:02:08.060
 

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:11.480
 

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:14.600
 

00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:17.680
 

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:19.680
 

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:22.380
 

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:25.980
 

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:28.760
 

00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:30.760
 

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:34.220
 

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:36.580
Woo-hoo!

00:02:36.580 --> 00:02:41.740
 

00:02:41.740 --> 00:02:43.540
 

00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:46.660
 

00:02:46.660 --> 00:02:50.240
 

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:54.580
 

00:02:54.580 --> 00:02:56.400
Woo-hoo-hoo!

00:02:56.400 --> 00:02:58.534
- The main difference when
having someone on the wing

00:02:58.534 --> 00:03:02.420
is that there is a significant amount
of drag from that person.

00:03:02.420 --> 00:03:06.220
It's a bit like flying an aircraft
with your gear and flaps down.

00:03:06.220 --> 00:03:10.000
You'll need more power to fly
and you need to check the trim as well,

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:14.881
because the people on the wing
provide a forward centre of gravity.

00:03:14.881 --> 00:03:17.700
- So at the end of the show,
we'll chat to everyone

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:20.009
and they'll tell us what they enjoyed.

00:03:20.009 --> 00:03:22.889
Usually, it's when we do the rolls
and we go upside down

00:03:22.889 --> 00:03:25.083
because they can't quite
believe that we're strapped on.

00:03:25.083 --> 00:03:26.513
From the air, you can't see our wire.

00:03:26.513 --> 00:03:28.638
It's all a bit of an illusion, so it looks
like we're not attached,

00:03:28.638 --> 00:03:30.260
and they're like, "Oh my gosh!"

00:03:30.260 --> 00:03:33.079
So it's a bit of a daredevil thing
they think is going on as well.

00:03:33.080 --> 00:03:34.580
- Kids come up to us all the time,

00:03:34.580 --> 00:03:36.720
especially young girls that are doing
gymnastics and think,

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:38.420
'oh my gosh, that's incredible',

00:03:38.420 --> 00:03:41.649
so it's really nice to feel like you're
a bit of a role model for people.

00:03:41.649 --> 00:03:43.984
- That is exhausting.

00:03:43.984 --> 00:03:48.936
That is one of the most
physically strenuous things

00:03:48.940 --> 00:03:51.640
I think I've ever done.

00:03:58.440 --> 00:03:59.960
Whoo!

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:06.720
Hahaha!

